I have no probems with death penalties for killing endangered species.
Not because of "moral" rights and such, but because I value snow
leapards, for example, more highly than people, in general.

With six billion blobs of allegedly human protoplasm out there, and
only a hundred or so of these rare animals, the choice is clear.

I'm thoroughly disgusted when I hear about African rhino carcasses
found with just the horns cut off, all because some Asians think it's
a cure for their sexual problems. I'm not sure what the solution is,
possibly further privatization of the lands the rhinos are on, or
perhaps even more novel methods involving tagging the animals,
tracking them, and establishing "protection rights."

In short, and I don't thing Dave would disagree with this, if "my"
rhino was about to be slaughtered for its horns, and I caught the
poachers, I'd have no problems shooting them on the spot.

I'm not arguing morality here, just the aesthetics Dave himself has
argued. Saving whales and tigers is a good thing, and I applaud such
recent developments as the marketing campaign for "dolphin-safe"
tuna...the free market appears to have worked quite well and also
created more public awareness without heavy-handed programs.

Having said this, I disliked the Brin novel intensely. As propaganda
for Brin's beliefs, it was terribly poorly done, IMHO. And the
characters were unmemorable...I could never keep them straight (but
then I was flipping the pages faster and faster as time went by).

