Date: Tue, 28 Dec 1993 23:05:36 -0500
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Speed of evolution/selection of traits in humans...

Jay R. Freeman discusses the question of measuring genetic vs environmental
contributions to intelligence. An important point often missed in
discussions of this question is that a statement such as "X% of the
variance of intelligence is due to heredity" is a description of a
particular population, not a general statement that will be true for any
population. If you hold genetic variability constant and increase the
variability of environment you will increase the contribution of
environment to intelligence. If you had a population entirely cloned from
one individual, 100% of the variation in intelligence would be due to
environment--because there would be no variation in genetics.

