Subject: Faical Features

        Romana is writing an essay about the social psychology of physical 
attractiveness...
 
Sounds interesting.  I'm especially curious about what determines the 
standards for facial beauty.  Perhaps someone has interesting information on 
that topic... Facial features seem much more subtle than other physical 
characteristics.  I have a guess about how facial beauty is measured, and I'm 
curious to hear comments (I haven't read anything about this topic and I may 
be totally wrong):
 
It seems to me that the standard for beauty (obviously it varies from person 
to person) in facial features is a weighted average of the features of the 
group of people that one is exposed to while growing up, where the average is 
weighted heavily in favor of people in power.  After all, variations in facial 
features have no obvious impact on survival, so any preferences seems highly 
arbitrary.  I would guess that if there were many more people with large noses 
in power, the standard for nose size would increase and people who are getting 
nosejobs right now would no longer wish to.  Even if a group is extremely 
successful (e.g. Jews), it must be large enough in order to have a major impact

on the weighted average.
 
I also imagine that standards for facial beauty are mostly "burnt" into 
children's minds by a certain age based upon their culture, and are probably 
much harder to change after that.  I wonder what that age is (maybe around 12 
or so?).  For example, if suddenly blacks became the top ten % or so of 
wealthiest Americans, most American adults would not appreciably change their 
automatic response to black facial features (although they might change many 
other aspects of their conscious assessment of blacks), whereas young children 
who are growing up now will probably have very different standards of facial 
beauty.
 
I wonder what effect racism has on such aesthetic evaluations of faces...  
I've heard that in Japan the prices of Caucasian prostitutes dropped as 
the relative economic status of the Japanese increased.  Probably 
such an effect is particularly pronounced in Japan because of its extreme, 
conscious racism, which had been tempered by appreciation for the greater 
economic clout of Western nations.  The other effects described above 
probably exist naturally even in groups of people that are not racist.  
It seems like a child developing a liking for certain foods while growing up.
 
For body features, certain qualities have obvious survival benefits, so the 
ideal should not be the average.  For example, greater height (within limits) 
is naturally considered attractive.
 
Are there parts of Africa that are still free from much Western cultural 
exposure?  If so, what would people there think about the facial features of 
Caucasian women who are considered very beautiful here?  Has anyone tried 
this?  I would guess that such Africans would think the women look bizarre 
(even aside from their ridiculously pale skin color).

