Date: Tue, 28 Dec 1993 20:47:50 -0800 (PST)
From: joh@netcom.com (John Oh)
Subject: Extropially Correct Welfare

A belated reply...
Regarding Jay Freeman's comments (why is he a squirrel?) about the benefits of 
certain kinds of welfare...  Certainly education is a great target for money 
if you plan to give money away.  Intelligence is such a highly leveraged 
resource, so if you spend your money on education wisely, you should certainly 
be able to generate a profit.  The question is how do you reap some of that 
profit?  I agree that the question deserves attention and I briefly thought 
about the topic several years ago.
 
My basic idea is that you can set up a contract to fund the education of a 
student for a number of years and then one could be repaid from the future 
earnings of the student.  It might be a good idea for a company to pay for the 
education rather than an individual, so the company could be guaranteed to 
reap the surplus benefit of employing that person for a certain amount of 
time.  Perhaps the education expenses could even be tax-deductible for a 
company.
 
Funding the educations could involve setting up your own schools or virtual 
schools rather than just paying for existing educational services or software.
 
If you screen for very bright children, you should be able to ensure a high 
probability that the students will gain the ability to easily generate large 
incomes in the future.  By age 15 or so, the students should be able to fund 
further education with part-time consulting work.  The less stringent your 
requirements for mental ability, the harder it will be to ensure a profit.
 
I think the education for the children should start early.  Unfortunately, the 
children won't be able to make informed decisions about contracts themselves.
I don't like the idea of parents making decisions that will bind a child to 
major obligations long in the future.  Perhaps the child could be given to 
option to back out up to a certain age.
 
The above idea might be especially suited to children outside the US (e.g. 
India or China).  Perhaps the cost of funding the education elsewhere could be 
lower than here, and it should be a lot easier to find many children 
with great aptitude but without access to proper educations.  Also, it might 
be easier to find families that care about education but can't afford what 
you are offering.  In the future, when these children enter the job market, 
the relative disadvantage of their locations will probably be much lower as 
the net will have grown so much.
 
By providing the above services on a (relatively) large scale, you may be 
able to offer good prices.
 
There are probably all sorts of legal problems (both in US and elsewhere) 
with requiring someone to work for you in the future or pay some 
portion of their income to you.
 
Aside from legal issues, maybe a company like Microsoft could try a plan like 
the above?  They should be able to start using talented children at a pretty 
young age, and they seem more interested in ability than paper credentials 
like college or graduate degrees.
 
What effect will cryptoanarchy have on the feasibility of an idea like the 
above?
 
J oh N


