Subject: IQ TESTS & MENSA

I didn't want recognition for being a brain... I already had that,
and it was often a little annoying.  I joined Mensa because
'normal' people bored me with their endless and witless discussion
of trivia.  I quit Mensa because 'intelligent' people bored me
with their endless and witless discussion of trivia.  I decided
that I had to make my own amusement and/or settle for the company
of good books, in the apparent absence of people willing and able
to discuss intelligently topics that _I considered_ of genuine
import and interest.  I also joined Mensa because I wanted to
be able to speak with people who would neither give me blank
stares, nor accuse me of trying to 'sound smart'.  I should
mention that I was somewhere between 10 and 15 years of age
at the time, and consequently could neither converse with
people of my own age (who in our rural area knew a lot about
milking machines but little else) nor adults (who considered me
a smartass kid [probably with some justice]).

I finally found what I was looking for in the engineering
workplace and on the Nets.  I wonder if the increased availability
of the Nets will have a truly beneficial effect upon kids in
my former position... being left without such a resource for
a few years forced me to develop some physical and social skills
and to create my own intellectual pursuits rather than be
titillated...
