Subject:  Tecno-Historicism

>Duncan Frissell <frissell@panix.com>
>
>Networks and computers have caused institutional downsizing in the US and 
>Europe not because people independently decided to downsize but because 
>the technology made that choice the most efficient arrangement.  The 
>Nation State and large corporations were formed in the first place because 
>of this sort of encounter between people's desires and the technological 
>and organizational realities.  Those realities are changing and so the 
>institutions will change.
>
>We are in for major institutional change even if not one person adopts one 
>new idea.  The ideas that are already in their heads are enough when you 
>mix them up with the new technology.

Duncan's claims are ambiguous at best, especially the one that technological
changes will "carry ideas along with them," which I edited out from the above
for brevity's sake.

The simple truth is that history is full of cases in which the technology
for revolutionary social change existed and wasn't implemented.  The ideas
weren't carried along.  What people already had in mind wasn't enough.  Some
examples I can toss of from the top of my head are: the steam engines the
Ancient Greeks used for toys, rather than trains; Congreve's rockets, which
inspired the line in the American National Anthem, the Star Spangled Banner:
"And the rockets' red glare..."  These were used in the war of 1812, but
Wellington put a stop to their R&D because he thought they couldn't be made
as reliable as other alternatives, even though Congreve knew he could.  Also
cars could have been developed at the beginning of the 19th century, but
the horse&buggy and railroad lobbies got the State to subsidize their
efforts and thus discourage the rise of this technology, which would have
outcompeted them.  Paul Johnson goes over these and other in his last book,
"The Birth of the Modern."  Faxes, TV, and many other inventions such as
those of Tesla weren't implemented for various reasons, including the lack
of memetic support.  Carroll Quigley (late Georgetown history prof. whose
students included Bill Clinton) does a good job of covering how the
Roman Empire had the tecnology for feudalism, but didn't implement it
because its instrument of expansion was slavery, which lead to the
distraction of its leaders, innovators, and entrepreneurs from the
capabilities of this new technology.

At worst, what underlies this is a form of historicism, techno-historicism,
which is no more immune from Popper's refutation of it because it says
that we can predict with certainty that technology is the sufficient case
of social change than any other kind of historicism.  We don't know what
we know.  We don't know what will have yet to discover.  What we will
discover will guide our actions in the future.  Thus, we don't know what
our future actions will be guided by - not with certainty.  We can get
pretty good ideas, make educated guesses in light of wisdom gained through
the study of history, but that's as good as it gets.

The two cases Duncan cites above also are ones where what people already
knew didn't suffice to cause the relevant changes.  Downsizing wouldn't
have happened the way it did if management literature hadn't been
written in which the designs of new organizational structures had been
exposed to managers.  The nation-state was one of many ideas about how
to deal with the threat of the Spanish-Habsburg Empire of Charles V,
which controlled the Austrian Empire, Spain, and most of the New World -
the greatest Empire of its time, and one of the greatest of all time.
The idea was that all "nations," that is, racial/linguistic/ethnic
groups ought to have their own States, rather than one big State
encompassing them all.  This was an instance of the "divide and conquer"
strategy.  It made the many different nations within the Spanish Empire
realize that their money was being stolen from them by the State and
given to people besides themselves.  This made them less willing to
tolerate taxation, which led them to one of the greatest succession
of taxpayer revolts in history.  Before the idea of the nation-state,
they put up with these taxes.

I renew my challenge: has there ever been any social change of the kind
and on the scale we're contemplating with technological change alone?
That is, without memetic change that wasn't caused by the technological
change?

This is like the challenges we often field from people who present us
with armed social conflict allegedly without States, where struggles to
establish or gain control over them are revealed by closer scrutiny.

